A||||7||deceler8||Laptop hard drive...is it bad ?||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||10-01-2001||10:00 PM||||And can it be fixed ?<P>My laptop gave out today. It suddenly stopped working, and when rebooted, would not get through Scandisk without lots of error messages. Something about too long file names that couldn't be cleared.<P>So I took the hard drive out and put it in my desktop to read it. Most of the file names were corrupted.<P>I'm going to assume I've lost all data on this machine, which is not a real issue as there's not much on it. I've gone through FDISK and deleted and reinstalled the DOS partition and formatted the drive, but the laptop won't read the drive, and putting the drive back in my desktop reveals some of the file names are corrupted again.<P>Any suggestions ? Is this drive trash, or can it be saved ?<P>I'll appreciate any help any of you can provide.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||10-01-2001||10:58 PM||||Try to fdisk and format the drive on your desktop.  Then see if you can copy files to and read from it.  Then put it in the laptop and see what you get.  The HDD may be ok, the laptop may be bad - bad connection or bad HDD controller on the laptop's system board.<P>What brand laptop?  Some manufacturers have downloadable diagnostics from their websites, like Compaq and IBM.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||10-02-2001||10:42 AM||||Hey Bill...you out there ?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||10-03-2001||02:56 AM||||if you need a replacement drive, email me, i have 10- 12 2.5' laptop drives, need to know manufacturer/model of laptop, and if possible, measure it from the side to see how many mm's it is high. fierostetz@home.com||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||10-03-2001||10:21 AM||||Most likely the drive had a translation table (drive manager) installed. This is usually needed when the drive is upgraded to larger than the bios can handel. When you ran FDISK you removed the manager and the laptop can no longer see the drive. What you will need to do is go to the drive manufacturers site and download the drive manager to another computer and create a bootable floppy disk.<BR>The way drive managers work is they put a translation table on the hard disk and then custom set a drive in the computers bios that way the computer can see the drive and read the translation table. <BR>All the drive managers can automaticly make changes to the system and even partition the drive and quick format it for you.<BR>I may have a few drive managers laying around but bear in mind that each manager is spacific to the drive but most use variations of EZDRIVE.<P>Here is the Western Digital <A HREF="http://www.westerndigital.com/service/ftp/dlgtools/EZmaker.exe" TARGET=_blank>EZDrive</A><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000005||deceler8||10-03-2001||07:35 PM||||Hey Stetz...you talkin' free ?<P>Any 2.5 inch drive less than 17mm will work, but the drive I'm replacing is a 12mm 1.0 gb drive.<P>If you're talking free, I'll pay shipping.<P>As to EZdrive, I'll give it a shot, but I'll check the Seagate site to see if they have a version for download.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
